#{[17592186180755 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\Delta$ with sides $S_1, S_2,$ and $S_3,$  shown below. Let's imagine that we traverse the boundary once in the counterclockwise direction starting from the origin.\n\n![](images/23/231dd5f96cd36d21940fcee894382edc9eabc91b0bf72bb4a8724c8444f76a8c.png)\n\nLet the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle, presented in the order shown in the diagram:\n![](images/34/34be6d7cd24938fb72976910cf3ad6a790eb93574915829f621da99f4aec3838.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/be/bed30fcc6a1003bee7f2f7888af9c301314b2aba351497db35cf2ee37710c0a1.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertices of the image are given by the columns of the above matrix. Drawing these vertices in the order shown, i.e.\n\n$$\n(1,-1) \\to (1,0) \\to (2,-1),\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture:\n\n![](images/d5/d514834e6e9f108f478002416e863cf9c98d00add230c2e9321664ea14e5db0e.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that the polygon $D$ is traversed in the *clockwise* direction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1065 7695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c717442d-1c85-597b-bfc9-3bacde35a1f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Image of a Polygon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180756 #edb/ref 1064 "An invertible affine transformation is **orientation-preserving** if it preserves the orientation of any polygon in the plane.\n\nTo understand this, let's go back to our triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)\n\nThis transformation does *not* preserve orientation because when we traverse $\\Delta$ *counterclockwise*, its image $D$ is traversed *clockwise.*\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation is orientation-preserving or not by examining the matrix $A$ of the associated linear map. We have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is defined as* \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Then, $\\mathbf{T}$ preserves orientation if*\n\n$$\n\\det(A) > 0,\n$$\n\n*and does not preserve orientation if*\n\n$$\n\\det(A) < 0.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to cases where $\\det(A) < 0$ as **orientation-reversing.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180756 #edb/ref 1065 7696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180756 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0444feba-dbdb-5165-942a-79da29a810e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180756 #edb/ref 1067 "Orientation-Preserving Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180760 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$  is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nSimilar to the case of linear transformations, the ** area scale factor** of $\\mathbf T$ is given by the determinant of $A.$\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation increases or decreases the area of a polygon $\\mathcal P$ by examining the area scale factor:\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|>1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *larger* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|=1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have the *same* area as $\\mathcal P.$\n\n- If $0 < |\\det(A)| < 1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *smaller* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\nNote that if $\\det(A) = 0,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ is not a polygon. We discuss this case in more detail in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2,$ from the previous discussion, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/7c/7c637211f31b2e8e49fb56f6e60573c47bada4fa36b6c6e0fa7474c3e4d106fc.svg)\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |-1| = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the area of triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ under $\\mathbf T$ in the $xy$-plane have the same area. \n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180760 #edb/ref 1065 7705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180760 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e306ae2c-f307-52ed-a9fc-9376bdfeaa2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180760 #edb/ref 1067 "The Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183667 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "af669ad0-ae47-5a9d-ab8c-914967692ad4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Power of a Nilpotent Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184159 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cc8dc1a1-9606-5b67-942e-219ab2ddfc1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Power of an Idempotent Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184160 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b51e8ff1-247b-5efd-a684-8fca6c064e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1045 "Nilpotent and Idempotent Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186184920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186184373 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6d30c0b5-be79-5a7a-808d-7678444ef5c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184918 #edb/ref 1061 16202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2ddcf91-fcb2-581a-af17-8b331c1212e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184919 #edb/ref 1061 16203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184919 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbec4efc-b26d-5e1e-b905-eda2f46094c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1061 17047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbfd42f1-c449-5a1c-89f3-22f0da76f2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1061 17077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfb120ef-6495-557c-acdc-22fecb070cee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184921 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1064 "A matrix is said to be **nilpotent** if some positive power of the matrix is the zero matrix. For example, the matrix\n\n$$B = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis nilpotent because its second power is the zero matrix:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =O\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo state the definition more precisely: a matrix with the property $B^k=O$ for a number $k>0$ is known as a **nilpotent** matrix, and the lowest number $k$ such that $B^k=O$ is called the **index.**\n\nTherefore, our matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1065 3130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "39a61f3b-3f47-5962-a20c-33867d6f1127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185315 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1064 "A matrix is said to be **idempotent**, when multiplied by itself, yields itself.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$B = [\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis idempotent because $B^2 = B$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot (-6)+(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =B\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1065 3131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3977ba53-51f9-53db-b487-753096fe77e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185316 #edb/ref 1067 "Idempotent Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256907 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ccab3c0-0254-5090-9c86-829a851b15b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256949 #edb/ref 1080 "(9,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256949 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256949 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "124ccea5-c558-54e8-931d-ac8f24d5e647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257179 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257179 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186257179 #edb/ref 